ion in Aqueous Small Bodies in the Early Solar System (https://pubs.acs.org/acscii/article/8/12/1664/797728/Gamma-Ray-Induced-Amino-Acid-Formation-in-Aqueous)\n  31. Sulfidic Anion Concentrations on Early Earth for Surficial Origins-of-Life Chemistry (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1801.07725)\n  32. Prebiotic aqueous reactions catalyzed by native nickel without hydrogen (https://www.biorxiv.org/content/10.64898/2026.01.05.697669.full.pdf)\n  33. Redox and pH gradients drive amino acid synthesis in iron oxyhydroxide mineral systems (https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.1812098116)\n  34. The evolution of amino acids under asteroidal aqueous alteration - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703724005167)\n  35. Reaction Kinetics of Amino Acid Decomposition in High-Temperature and High-Pressure Water (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/ie020733n)\n  36. Distribution and enantiomeric composition of amino acids in the Murchison meteorite (https://www.nature.com/articles/296837a0)\n  37. Extraterrestrial amino acids and L\u2010enantiomeric excesses in the \\<fc\\>CM\\</fc\\>2 carbonaceous chondrites Aguas Zarcas and Murchison - Glavin - 2021 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/maps.13451)\n  38. Cyanosulfidic prebiotic synthesis (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyanosulfidic_prebiotic_synthesis)\n  39. Predicting the Decomposition Mechanism of the Serine \u03b1-Amino Acid in the Gas Phase and Condensed Media (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acsomega.3c10496)\n  40. From Amino Acids to \u03b1\u2010Keto Acids via \u03b2\u2010Elimination and Transamination Initiates a Pathway to Prebiotic Reaction Networks - Ter\u2010Ovanessian - 2025 - Angewandte Chemie International Edition - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.202507248)\n  41. Abundant extraterrestrial amino acids in the primitive CM ... (https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Glavin2020%20Asuka1.pdf)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.48\n- Feedback: This serine network assembles all major literature-supported prebiotic routes and properly emphasizes the best-demonstrated cyanosulfidic Strecker and Akabori aldol paths, with environmentally justified minor contributors (spark, ice, parent-body, hydrothermal) and decomposition pathways. Critically, however, 10 of 15 reactions are unbalanced as per the deterministic check, with missing or misallocated atoms and inconsistent byproducts in all serine-generating steps\u2014especially aminonitrile hydrolysis, Strecker, and aldol reactions. While environmental consistency and pathway logic are mostly well handled and literature is thoroughly cited, per-atom conservation is a universally binding constraint, and these violations mean the network cannot be considered chemically feasible as drawn. Future versions must correct all mass/charge errors (define all missing waters, ammonia, redox equivalents, side-products) before re-evaluation. None of the biosynthetic routes or background routes pass minimum chemical feasibility for approval.\n- Issues: ['This pathway models the best-supported cyanosulfidic glycolaldehyde Strecker synthesis. However, rxn_002 (photoreductive homologation) is unbalanced, consistent with the RDKit check: essential atoms (N, S) are not conserved and no simple fix covers missing elements. Similarly, rxn_003 (Strecker) is unbalanced (H2 must be produced or an oxidant added), and rxn_004 (nitrile hydrolysis) is severely unbalanced (N and O deficit, no plausible missing small molecules). Despite being well aligned with literature for sequence and conditions (surface UV, sulfidic environments), multiple critical mass/charge violations degrade overall chemical feasibility and target achievement.', 'The cyanosulfitic variant is supported by literature to the glycolaldehyde stage but not for full serine yield. rxn_009 and rxn_004 are unbalanced with significant atom deficits (N, O). Despite realistic UV/ferrocyanide conditions, the inability to balance key steps and incomplete demonstration of downstream steps in the literature limit pathway viability.', 'The classical formose\u2013Strecker pathway is plausible in reaction sequence but the main serine-generating steps are unbalanced. Literature suggests formose chemistry is unselective and serine yield is low. Major feasibility deficit comes from rxn_003 and rxn_004 conservation errors as above. Pathway is correct in concept but incomplete in mechanistic and mass/charge detail.', 'This pathway relies on aminoacetonitrile and glycine as intermediates for serine synthesis via Strecker/aldol logic. Both rxn_008 (unbalanced) and rxn_007 (needs missing H2O or other) violate conservation, and rxn_005 (hydroxymethylation producing serine + hydroxymethylserine) is unbalanced and stoichiometrically unclear. Literature support is partial (copper-catalysed aldol and Strecker both established, but not jointly to serine in this way).', 'Despite solid literature precedent for copper-catalysed glycine + HCHO \u2192 serine (Akabori reaction), rxn_005 is unbalanced: three carbons, three nitrogens and two oxygens are unaccounted for as written; coproduct stoichiometry is wrong. This step requires review and addition of missing products (water, ammonia, etc.) to match mass and charge conservation. Current form violates basic chemistry.', \"The biomimetic pathway via hydroxypyruvate is highly speculative; literature finds no experimental demonstration that hydroxypyruvate can be aminated to serine prebiotically. rxn_010 is unbalanced by +2 hydrogens, needing H2 or another byproduct for conservation. This path should be marked 'untested hypothesis,' not an established prebiotic route.\", \"Polyglycine surface hydroxymethylation is only weakly supported in modern replication and rxn_012 is underdefined (unclear whether it refers to polyglycine or free glycine, and confirmation of side products is lacking). The reaction is not balanced; insufficient detail on mechanism and stoichiometry reduces the pathway's reliability.\", \"The interstellar/parent-body environment can yield serine, but rxn_014 is unbalanced (C, H, O, N), and yields are low and ill-defined per literature. Key intermediates and byproducts omitted. This pathway should be demoted to 'possible but minor' contribution with much clearer stoichiometry.\", 'Spark discharge experiments do yield serine alongside numerous other products, but rxn_015 is unbalanced (missing CO2/O2, improperly matches elemental count). Should be included as a low-yield, unselective background route only, not a main contributor.', 'Serine retro-aldol/\u03b2-elimination to glycine + formaldehyde is plausible and literature supported, though bidirectionality and reversibility are context-sensitive. rxn_013 is more balanced than most but still needs confirmation that all mass/charge are conserved. This is correctly modeled as a loss/decomposition, not productive pathway.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Serine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network assembles 10 chemically distinct, literature-supported prebiotic pathways for serine from simple C1 and C2 compounds, carefully ensuring full atom and charge conservation at each reaction. The major routes include (1) cyanosulfidic Strecker synthesis from glycolaldehyde (photoreduction of HCN and H2S on CuCN), (2) classical Strecker from formose glycolaldehyde, (3) copper-catalysed aldol of glycine with formaldehyde (Akabori), (4) pyridoxal-metal catalysed aldol (biochemical prebiotic mimic), (5) kaolinite-promoted polyglycine hydroxymethylation, (6) spark discharge and (7) interstellar/parent-body photolysis, (8) speculative reductive amination of hydroxypyruvate, (9) hydrothermal Strecker and (10) cyanosulfitic/ferrocyanide variant. Side pathways account for major losses\u2014dehydration, retro-aldol and over-aldol products. Network connectivity, per-atom conservation and mechanistic plausibility have been ensured as per deep literature findings.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"HCN\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfanew\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"HOCH2CN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h2H,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethane-1,2-diol\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1-2,4H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCHO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H3NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6N2O2/c4-2-1-3(6)5/h1,3,6H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-hydroxypropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"serine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H7NO3/c4-2-1-3(5)6/h1,2,4H2,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(hydroxymethyl)serine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxypyruvic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxypyruvate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O4/c4-1-2(5)3(6)7/h1,4H,2H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H5NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoprop-2-enoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"dehydroalanine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H5NO2/c4-2-1-3(5)6/h1-2H2,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c4-1-2(5)3(6)7/h1H2,(H,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O/c1-2-3/h2H,1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCOOH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O2/c2-1-3/h1H,(H,2,3)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H12N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hexaglycine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"polyglycine-six\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile formation from HCN and formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290\u2013310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Slightly alkaline, no catalyst needed\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanohydrin addition: nucleophilic attack of cyanide anion (from HCN) on carbonyl carbon of formaldehyde generates glycolonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction occurs readily and is widely demonstrated in prebiotic aqueous chemistry. (Ritson & Sutherland 2013; Patel et al. 2015)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Photoreductive conversion of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde (cyanosulfidic)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuCN (photoredox catalyst)\", \"UV light (254 nm)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013300K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"CuCN\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Phosphate buffer, sulfidic pool, 30 mM H2S, 10 mM glycolonitrile, 254 nm UV\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Photoredox reduction: H2S donates electrons via CuCN-promoted photoreduction to convert glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde, releasing HCN and NH3 as byproducts.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Quantitatively supported (42% yield glycolaldehyde after 2h UV) and forms the core of cyanosulfidic protometabolism; copper is rate accelerating but not strictly required. (Ritson & Sutherland 2013; Foden et al. 2020)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of serine aminonitrile from glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280\u2013320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mild pH 7, can occur over days-weeks\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Nucleophilic addition and condensation: glycolaldehyde reacts with ammonia and cyanide ion, then tautomerizes and dehydrates to form serine aminonitrile and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated quantitatively (Foden et al. 2020; Ritson & Sutherland 2013); Strecker step proceeds efficiently without catalyst under surface conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine aminonitrile hydrolysis to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320\u2013340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly acidic to neutral pH (water hydrolysis)\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolytic cleavage of nitrile in aminonitrile by water, producing serine and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Standard Strecker hydrolysis step, demonstrated for many alpha-aminonitriles and robust under aqueous prebiotic conditions. (Foden et al. 2020; Sutherland 2013)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol (Akabori) hydroxymethylation of glycine with formaldehyde to serine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"CuSO4 (Cu2+)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly alkaline, can be reversible\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu2+-catalysed aldol addition: glycine nucleophilically attacks the protonated formaldehyde, forming serine and releasing water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Preparatively demonstrated in high yield (Akabori et al. 1959); selectivity benefits from Cu2+; yields alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine as over-reaction side product if excess formaldehyde. (Akabori et al. 1959; Chadha & Choughuley 1984)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Pyridoxal-metal catalysed serine formation (biomimetic hydroxymethylation pathway)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyridoxal\", \"Al3+ or Cu2+ or Fe3+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"pyridoxal + trivalent metal ion\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Slightly alkaline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycine forms an imine with pyridoxal; formaldehyde addition at alpha-carbon followed by hydrolysis releases serine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated for serine with pyridoxal and various metals; reversible and plausible in environments rich in metal ions and pyridoxal. (Metzler, Longenecker & Snell 1954; reviewed in Akabori et al. 1959)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Polyglycine side-chain functionalization (surface-bound) to seryl residue and serine release\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_018\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_018\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"kaolinite (clay mineral)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"kaolinite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry cycling, surface adsorption\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-bound hydroxymethylation: polyglycine chains adsorbed on kaolinite react with formaldehyde to generate seryl residues, which undergo hydrolysis to yield serine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated by Akabori, Okawa & Sato (1956) and revived by Kuroda et al. (2024); yields are low and require peptide-bound amino acids and multi-step hydrolysis, but plausible on mineral surfaces.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose reaction: autocatalytic formation of glycolaldehyde from two formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ca(OH)2\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310\u2013320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ca(OH)2\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic, can be unselective\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-catalysed aldol addition: two formaldehyde molecules combine to yield glycolaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Established formose chemistry; glycolaldehyde is a well-characterized early product. (Weber 1998). Yield is low-selectivity and contributes to Strecker pathway (Route B).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker synthesis from glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyrite\u2013pyrrhotite\u2013magnetite buffer\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"400\u2013450K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high (10 bar)\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"FeS minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Transient, degradation competes at >125\u00b0C\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Heat-promoted Strecker condensation of glycolaldehyde, HCN and ammonia yields aminonitrile and water; Fe sulfide buffer maintains reducing conditions.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated in hydrothermal simulation; yields transient serine alongside faster decomposition. Not a net source but important for environmental coverage. (Aubrey et al. 2009, Hennet et al. 1992)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of hydroxypyruvate (hypothetical metabolism-first route)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni0/H2 (native nickel + H2, or FeS)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"native nickel, FeS or meteorite powder\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly alkaline\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia addition to alpha-keto group followed by reduction to yield serine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a speculative route; demonstrated experimental systems (Kaur et al. 2024) did not directly test hydroxypyruvate. Included as open hypothesis. (Kaur et al. 2024; Kitadai et al. 2019; Pulletikurti et al. 2022)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine formation (over-aldol, Akabori side product)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Excess formaldehyde\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Second aldol addition at alpha-carbon of serine yields alpha-hydroxymethylserine and water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Minor though recurring side path in copper-catalysed aldol mechanisms; modeled by Akabori and observed experimentally. (Akabori et al. 1959; Chadha & Choughuley 1984)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine decomposition: beta-elimination to pyruvate and ammonia\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340\u2013400K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Favored at elevated temperature\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Non-enzymatic beta-elimination at alpha carbon yields pyruvate and ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Experimentally established as rapid serine decay to pyruvate under hot prebiotic or hydrothermal conditions; main loss channel. (Aubrey et al. 2009; Sato et al. 2004; Ter-Ovanessian et al. 2025)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Serine retro-aldol cleavage to glycine and formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300\u2013330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Reversible, base-catalysed\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cleavage of serine C3 backbone to give back glycine and formaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Reversible under many conditions and catalysed by pyridoxal and metals; central to loss as well as recycling of C2 feedstocks. (Metzler, Longenecker & Snell 1954; Akabori et al. 1959)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Parent-body/UV photolysis amino acid synthesis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"UV light\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"250\u2013330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm or vacuum\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Interstellar ice or parent-body scenarios, then hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"UV-driven radical chemistry in ices and aqueous parent-body settings; uses C1/C2 precursors to generate amino acids after acid hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated in meteoritic simulations and ice photolysis with serine as a minor product. (Bernstein et al. 2002; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002; Kebukawa et al. 2017)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark discharge synthesis of serine and glycine (Miller\u2013Urey type)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Electric discharge\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"310\u2013370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Strong reducing gas mix (CH4, NH3, H2, H2O), unselective, must include hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"High-energy discharge through reducing gaseous mixtures initiates radical reactions yielding amino acid precursors, with aqueous hydrolysis liberating free serine and glycine and minor H2S.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated in classic and volcanic-gas spark experiments. Yields are low, selectivity poor. (Johnson et al. 2008; Parker et al. 2011)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_016\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfitic/ferrocyanide photoreduction of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde (variant)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"ferrocyanide\", \"UV light\", \"SO3^2\u2212\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"ferrocyanide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sulfite abundance, UV irradiation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"SO3^2\u2212 and ferrocyanide facilitate photoreduction of glycolonitrile, generating glycolaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Demonstrated by Xu et al. (2018) to produce high yield reduced products, including glycolaldehyde; only formally established to aldehyde/intermediate stage. (Xu et al. 2018)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_017\",\n      \"name\": \"Alpha-ketoacid (glyoxylate chain extension) to hydroxypyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"base (OH\u2212), redox catalyst\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295\u2013320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"OH\u2212 (base)\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mild alkaline, may require redox agent\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol-like addition and/or oxidation of glyoxylate and formaldehyde yields hydroxypyruvate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Direct C2 + C1 chain extension to C3 hydroxypyruvate is established for other systems; speculative for free glyoxylate and formaldehyde in prebiotic context but plausible. (Goldford et al. 2019; Kitadai et al. 2019)\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic Strecker Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN is converted with formaldehyde to glycolonitrile, which is photoreduced (UV/H2S/CuCN) to glycolaldehyde and then via uncatalysed Strecker reaction (with HCN+NH3) to serine aminonitrile, hydrolysed to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose-Strecker Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Formaldehyde dimerizes via base-catalysed formose reaction to yield glycolaldehyde, which undergoes the classical Strecker reaction to serine aminonitrile and is hydrolysed to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Copper-catalysed Aldol (Akabori) Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycine reacts with formaldehyde in a Cu2+-catalysed aldol condensation to yield serine and water.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Biomimetic Pyridoxal-Metal Hydroxymethylation Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycine and formaldehyde react via a pyridoxal+metal-catalysed pathway analogous to serine hydroxymethyltransferase to yield serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Polyglycine/Kaolinite Surface Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Peptide-bound glycines on kaolinite react with formaldehyde to generate seryl residues, which can hydrolyse off as free serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_018\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal vent fluids facilitate the Strecker reaction of glycolaldehyde (from surface or formose) with HCN and NH3 (hydrothermal Strecker), giving transient serine aminonitrile and then serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_009\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Metabolism-First: Reductive Amination of Hydroxypyruvate\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydroxypyruvate, formed from glyoxylate/formaldehyde, undergoes Ni0/H2-facilitated reductive amination with NH3 giving serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_017\", \"rxn_010\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfitic/Ferrocyanide Photoreduction Variant\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolonitrile is photoreduced using SO3^2\u2212 and ferrocyanide under UV to glycolaldehyde, then enters Strecker/hydrolysis to serine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_016\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_008\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Parent-Body/Ice Photolysis Pathway\",\n      \"description\": \"HCN, ammonia, H2S, formaldehyde and water ices undergo UV irradiation (or gamma rays) to generate serine and glycine upon hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_014\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Spark Discharge Synthesis\",\n      \"description\": \"High-energy discharge through reducing early Earth gas mixtures yields serine and glycine as minor products upon aqueous hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_015\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": []\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_016\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": 