e generates glycolonitrile.", "reasoning": "This reaction occurs readily and is widely demonstrated in prebiotic aqueous chemistry. (Ritson & Sutherland 2013; Patel et al. 2015)"}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Photoreductive conversion of glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde (cyanosulfidic)", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_003", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuCN (photoredox catalyst)", "UV light (254 nm)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295\u2013300K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "CuCN", "aqueous": true, "other": "Phosphate buffer, sulfidic pool, 30 mM H2S, 10 mM glycolonitrile, 254 nm UV"}, "mechanism": "Photoredox reduction: H2S donates electrons via CuCN-promoted photoreduction to convert glycolonitrile to glycolaldehyde, releasing HCN and NH3 as byproducts.", "reasoning": "Quantitatively supported (42% yield glycolaldehyde after 2h UV) and forms the core of cyanosulfidic protometabolism; copper is rate accelerating but not strictly required. (Ritson & Sutherland 2013; Foden et al. 2020)"}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Strecker synthesis of serine aminonitrile from glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "280\u2013320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mild pH 7, can occur over days-weeks"}, "mechanism": "Nucleophilic addition and condensation: glycolaldehyde reacts with ammonia and cyanide ion, then tautomerizes and dehydrates to form serine aminonitrile and water.", "reasoning": "Demonstrated quantitatively (Foden et al. 2020; Ritson & Sutherland 2013); Strecker step proceeds efficiently without catalyst under surface conditions."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Serine aminonitrile hydrolysis to serine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_010", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "320\u2013340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly acidic to neutral pH (water hydrolysis)"}, "mechanism": "Hydrolytic cleavage of nitrile in aminonitrile by water, producing serine and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Standard Strecker hydrolysis step, demonstrated for many alpha-aminonitriles and robust under aqueous prebiotic conditions. (Foden et al. 2020; Sutherland 2013)"}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Aldol (Akabori) hydroxymethylation of glycine with formaldehyde to serine", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["CuSO4 (Cu2+)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline, can be reversible"}, "mechanism": "Cu2+-catalysed aldol addition: glycine nucleophilically attacks the protonated formaldehyde, forming serine and releasing water.", "reasoning": "Preparatively demonstrated in high yield (Akabori et al. 1959); selectivity benefits from Cu2+; yields alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine as over-reaction side product if excess formaldehyde. (Akabori et al. 1959; Chadha & Choughuley 1984)"}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Pyridoxal-metal catalysed serine formation (biomimetic hydroxymethylation pathway)", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": ["pyridoxal", "Al3+ or Cu2+ or Fe3+"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295\u2013310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "pyridoxal + trivalent metal ion", "aqueous": true, "other": "Slightly alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Glycine forms an imine with pyridoxal; formaldehyde addition at alpha-carbon followed by hydrolysis releases serine.", "reasoning": "Demonstrated for serine with pyridoxal and various metals; reversible and plausible in environments rich in metal ions and pyridoxal. (Metzler, Longenecker & Snell 1954; reviewed in Akabori et al. 1959)"}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Polyglycine side-chain functionalization (surface-bound) to seryl residue and serine release", "inputs": ["mol_018", "mol_006", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_018"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["kaolinite (clay mineral)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "kaolinite", "aqueous": true, "other": "Wet-dry cycling, surface adsorption"}, "mechanism": "Surface-bound hydroxymethylation: polyglycine chains adsorbed on kaolinite react with formaldehyde to generate seryl residues, which undergo hydrolysis to yield serine.", "reasoning": "Demonstrated by Akabori, Okawa & Sato (1956) and revived by Kuroda et al. (2024); yields are low and require peptide-bound amino acids and multi-step hydrolysis, but plausible on mineral surfaces."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Formose reaction: autocatalytic formation of glycolaldehyde from two formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ca(OH)2"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310\u2013320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Ca(OH)2", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly basic, can be unselective"}, "mechanism": "Base-catalysed aldol addition: two formaldehyde molecules combine to yield glycolaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Established formose chemistry; glycolaldehyde is a well-characterized early product. (Weber 1998). Yield is low-selectivity and contributes to Strecker pathway (Route B)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrothermal Strecker synthesis from glycolaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["pyrite\u2013pyrrhotite\u2013magnetite buffer"], "conditions": {"temperature": "400\u2013450K", "pressure": "high (10 bar)", "catalyst": "FeS minerals", "aqueous": true, "other": "Transient, degradation competes at >125\u00b0C"}, "mechanism": "Heat-promoted Strecker condensation of glycolaldehyde, HCN and ammonia yields aminonitrile and water; Fe sulfide buffer maintains reducing conditions.", "reasoning": "Demonstrated in hydrothermal simulation; yields transient serine alongside faster decomposition. Not a net source but important for environmental coverage. (Aubrey et al. 2009, Hennet et al. 1992)"}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Reductive amination of hydroxypyruvate (hypothetical metabolism-first route)", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_002", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_010"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["Ni0/H2 (native nickel + H2, or FeS)"], "conditions": {"temperature": "295\u2013320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "native nickel, FeS or meteorite powder", "aqueous": true, "other": "Mildly alkaline"}, "mechanism": "Ammonia addition to alpha-keto group followed by reduction to yield serine.", "reasoning": "This is a speculative route; demonstrated experimental systems (Kaur et al. 2024) did not directly test hydroxypyruvate. Included as open hypothesis. (Kaur et al. 2024; Kitadai et al. 2019; Pulletikurti et al. 2022)"}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Alpha-(hydroxymethyl)serine formation (over-aldol, Akabori side product)", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu2+"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Excess formaldehyde"}, "mechanism": "Second aldol addition at alpha-carbon of serine yields alpha-hydroxymethylserine and water.", "reasoning": "Minor though recurring side path in copper-catalysed aldol mechanisms; modeled by Akabori and observed experimentally. (Akabori et al. 1959; Chadha & Choughuley 1984)"}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Serine decomposition: beta-elimination to pyruvate and ammonia", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "340\u2013400K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Favored at elevated temperature"}, "mechanism": "Non-enzymatic beta-elimination at alpha carbon yields pyruvate and ammonia.", "reasoning": "Experimentally established as rapid serine decay to pyruvate under hot prebiotic or hydrothermal conditions; main loss channel. (Aubrey et al. 2009; Sato et al. 2004; Ter-Ovanessian et al. 2025)"}, {"id": "rxn_013", "name": "Serine retro-aldol cleavage to glycine and formaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_006"], "environment": "Biochemical", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300\u2013330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Reversible, base-catalysed"}, "mechanism": "Cleavage of serine C3 backbone to give back glycine and formaldehyde.", "reasoning": "Reversible under many conditions and catalysed by pyridoxal and metals; central to loss as well as recycling of C2 feedstocks. (Metzler, Longenecker & Snell 1954; Akabori et al. 1959)"}, {"id": "rxn_014", "name": "Parent-body/UV photolysis amino acid synthesis", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_010"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["UV light"], "conditions": {"temperature": "250\u2013330K", "pressure": "1 atm or vacuum", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": false, "other": "Interstellar ice or parent-body scenarios, then hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "UV-driven radical chemistry in ices and aqueous parent-body settings; uses C1/C2 precursors to generate amino acids after acid hydrolysis.", "reasoning": "Demonstrated in meteoritic simulations and ice photolysis with serine as a minor product. (Bernstein et al. 2002; Mu\u00f1oz Caro et al. 2002; Kebukawa et al. 2017)"}, {"id": "rxn_015", "name": "Spark discharge synthesis of serine and glycine (Miller\u2013Urey type)", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_010", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_009", "mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Electric discharge"], "conditions": {"temperature":